== Chapter VIII

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The second general rule proposed — Without sincerity in mortifying every lust, no lust will be mortified — Partial mortification always comes from a corrupt principle — The perplexity of temptation from a lust is often a chastening for other negligences.]

==== Second Rule: Sincerity in a Universal Obedience

#smallcaps[The] second principle I propose for this purpose is this:

#emph[Without sincerity and diligence in obedience in all things, no mortification of any one perplexing lust is to be obtained.]

The first rule concerned the person; this one concerns the thing itself. Let me explain this position a little.

A man finds that some lust brings him into the condition described before. It is powerful, strong, and turbulent; it leads him captive, harasses him, disquiets him, and takes away his peace. He cannot bear it. So he sets himself against it, prays against it, groans under it, and sighs to be delivered. But in the meantime, perhaps, he is loose and negligent in other duties: in constant communion with God, in reading, prayer, and meditation, and in other ways that are not of the same kind as the lust that troubles him. Let not that man think he will ever arrive at the mortification of the lust he is perplexed with. This is a condition that often befalls men in their pilgrimage. The Israelites, under a sense of their sin, drew near to God with great diligence and earnestness, with fasting and prayer (Isa 58). Much is said of their earnestness in the work. Verse 2: “Day after day they seek Me and delight to know My ways. They ask Me for righteous judgments; they delight in the nearness of God.” But God rejects it all. Their fast is a remedy that will not heal them. The reason given for it (verses 5–7) is that they were partial in this duty. They attended to it diligently, but in other duties they were negligent and careless. Take a man who has a “running sore” (it is the Scripture’s expression) that comes from an unhealthy state of body, brought on by intemperance and bad diet. Let him apply himself with all the diligence and skill he can to #emph[the cure of his sore]. If he leaves #emph[the general state of his body] in its disorders, his labor and trouble will be in vain. So will the attempts of the man who tries to stop a bloody flow of sin and filth in his soul, but is not equally careful of his whole spiritual health and constitution. For:

(1.) This kind of effort at mortification comes from a #emph[corrupt principle], ground, and foundation, so it will never come to a good end. The true and acceptable principles of mortification will be dealt with later. Hatred of sin as sin, and not only as something galling or disquieting, and a sense of the love of Christ in the cross, lie at the bottom of all true spiritual mortification. Now, it is certain that the effort I am speaking of comes from #emph[self-love]. You set yourself with all diligence and earnestness to mortify some lust or sin. What is the reason for it? It disquiets you. It has taken away your peace. It fills your heart with sorrow, trouble, and fear. You have no rest because of it. Yes; but, friend, you have neglected prayer or reading. You have been vain and loose in your conduct in other things, things not of the same nature as the lust that perplexes you. These are no less sins and evils than the ones you groan under. Jesus Christ bled for them too. Why do you not set yourself against them too? If you hated sin as sin, every evil way, you would be no less watchful against everything that grieves and disquiets the Spirit of God than against what grieves and disquiets your own soul. It is plain that you fight against #emph[sin] only because of the #emph[trouble] it gives you. If your conscience could be quiet under it, you would let it alone. If it did not disquiet you, it would not be disquieted by you. Now, can you think that God will join in with such hypocritical efforts? That his Spirit will ever bear witness to the treachery and falseness of your spirit? Do you think he will relieve you of what perplexes you, so that you may be free for what grieves him no less? No. God says, “Here is a man I would never hear from again if he could be rid of this lust. Let him wrestle with this, or he is lost.” Let no man think he will do his own work if he will not do God’s. God’s work consists in #emph[universal obedience]\; to be freed from the present perplexity is only their own. That is why the apostle says, “Let us cleanse ourselves from everything that defiles body and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God” (2 Cor 7:1). If we will do anything, we must do everything. So what is accepted is not merely an intense opposition to this or that particular lust. It is a humble frame and temper of heart in all things, watchful over every evil and for the doing of every duty.

(2.) How do you know that God has not allowed the lust that perplexes you to gain strength in you and power over you, to chasten you for your other negligences and your general lukewarmness in walking before him? Or at least to awaken you to consider your ways, so that you may make a thorough work and change in your whole course of walking with him?

The rage and dominance of a particular lust is commonly the fruit and result of a careless, negligent course in general. That is so for two reasons:

\[1.\] As its #emph[natural effect], if I may put it so. Lust, as I showed in general, lies in the heart of every man, even the best, as long as he lives. And do not think the Scripture speaks in vain when it says that lust is subtle, cunning, and crafty, that it seduces, entices, fights, and rebels. While a man keeps a diligent watch over his heart, its root and fountain, while above all keeping he keeps his heart, from which flow the springs of life and death, lust withers and dies in it. But suppose that through negligence it bursts out in some particular direction. It finds a passage to the thoughts through the affections, and from them and through them perhaps breaks out into open sin in his conduct. Then its strength bears in the direction it has found, and it presses mainly that way. Having found a passage, it then harasses and disquiets him, and it is not easily restrained. So a man may perhaps have to wrestle in sorrow all his days with what a strict and universal watch might easily have prevented.

\[2.\] As I said, God often allows it in order to #emph[chasten] our other negligences. With wicked men, he gives them up to #emph[one] sin as the judgment of another, #emph[a greater as the punishment of a lesser], or to one that will hold them more firmly and securely, for a sin they might perhaps have been delivered from.#footnote[Rom 1:26.] In the same way, even with his own people, he may leave them, and does leave them, at times to some vexing disorder, either to prevent or to cure some other evil. So the messenger of Satan was let loose on Paul, to keep him “from becoming conceited” because of the “surpassingly great revelations.”#footnote[2 Cor 12:7.] Was it not a correction of Peter’s vain confidence that he was left to deny his Master? Now, if this is the state and condition of lust when it prevails, that God often allows it to prevail, at least to warn us and humble us, perhaps to chasten and correct us for our generally loose and careless walking, is it possible that the #emph[effect] should be removed while the #emph[cause] continues? That the #emph[particular] lust should be mortified while the #emph[general] course is not reformed? So the man who would really, thoroughly, and acceptably mortify any disquieting lust must take care to be equally diligent in every part of obedience. And let him know that every lust, every neglect of duty, is a burden to God, though only one is a burden to the man himself.#footnote[Isa 43:24.] As long as there is treachery in the heart, ready to indulge any negligence and not pressing on to all perfection in obedience, the soul is #emph[weak], since it does not give faith its whole work; and #emph[selfish], since it thinks more of the trouble of sin than of its filth and guilt; and it lives under a constant #emph[provocation] of God. So it may not expect any encouraging outcome in any spiritual duty it undertakes, much less in this one we are considering, which requires another principle and frame of spirit to accomplish it.
